{#transclusion-document-identity}
# Transclusion Is Easy Until Two Documents Become One

Including one document inside another looks deceptively simple. Find a marker,
read some text, replace the marker, and run the markup parser. That model works
right up to the moment both documents contain a heading called “Introduction,”
a footnote named `note`, or an explicit identifier such as `#overview`.

At that point, inclusion stops being a text-substitution feature. It becomes a
document-composition problem.

Carve calls this feature /file inclusion/, or *transclusion*: source from another
Carve or text resource is placed into the document at the include site. Its
syntax is compact:

```carve
{{ chapters/security.crv }}
{{ chapters/security.crv #threat-model }}
{{ chapters/security.crv @shift:auto }}
```

The important part is not the braces. It is the contract around them. A useful
transclusion system must answer questions that a naïve preprocessor can avoid
only until production content exposes them:

- Which identifiers belong to the final document?
- Which names remain local to the included file?
- What happens when two files claim the same identifier?
- Do references follow a renamed target?
- When are footnotes collected and numbered?
- Does including a chapter change its heading hierarchy?
- Can diagnostics still point to the original file?
- Can an editor discover every dependency, including a missing one?

Those questions matter whether the source is a file, configuration entry,
database row, CMS record, object-storage key, or approved HTTP response. Storage
is an implementation detail. Identity is a semantic concern.

{#why-plain-concatenation-breaks}
## Why plain concatenation breaks

Imagine a parent and a reusable chapter:

```carve
%% parent.crv
{#overview}
## Overview

{{ child.crv }}
```

```carve
%% child.crv
{#overview}
## Overview

See </#overview>.
```

After blind substitution, the assembled HTML wants two elements with
`id="overview"`. HTML does not provide useful duplicate-ID semantics. A fragment
such as `#overview`, DOM lookup, accessibility relationship, or script selector
can reach the wrong element. Which one wins may depend on the browser API or
consumer.

Silently dropping the second identifier is not enough. The cross-reference in
the child was written to reach the child’s heading. If the heading is renamed
but the reference is not, the document is unique but incorrect.

The same problem appears in less obvious namespaces:

- Two included files define `[^note]`.
- Two headings generate the same automatic slug.
- Two figures, equations, citations, or glossary entries share a key.
- Two components expose the same ARIA relationship target.
- A table of contents is built before inclusion and misses child headings.
- A child’s relative link is resolved against the parent’s directory.

A preprocessor can solve these problems, but only by becoming aware of the
markup grammar and the assembled document. At that point it is no longer “just
text replacement.” It is a composition engine.

{#identity-has-scopes}
## Identity has scopes

The crucial design decision is that not every name should be global.

Carve divides relevant names by whether another file can observe them.

{#document-visible-identities}
### Document-visible identities

Explicit heading identifiers and footnote labels are visible in the assembled
document. A link can target a heading brought in by another file, and footnotes
are collected and numbered for the final document. These identities therefore
need document-wide uniqueness.

Carve resolves collisions deterministically:

1. The first occurrence in expanded document order keeps its name.
2. A later collision receives the smallest available numeric suffix: `-2`,
   then `-3`, and so on.
3. References belonging to the renamed target follow the rename.
4. Every explicit rename emits a warning with source provenance.

The result is stable and debuggable. “Stable” matters for caches, tests, deep
links, incremental builds, and collaboration: the same inputs and include order
must produce the same identities.

There is still an unavoidable consequence. Moving an include earlier in the
document can change which duplicate keeps the unsuffixed identifier. Explicit
collisions are therefore warnings, not an invisible convenience. Authors who
need durable public fragments should assign genuinely unique IDs rather than
depend on collision order.

{#automatic-heading-identifiers}
### Automatic heading identifiers

Repeated heading text is normal. A book may contain “Overview” in every
chapter. Treating each generated slug as an author error would create warning
noise precisely where reuse is most valuable.

Carve therefore handles automatic heading identifiers through the document’s
ordinary slug de-duplication. The first `Overview` gets its normal slug, and
later headings receive numeric suffixes. Unlike an explicit-ID collision, this
does not warn because the author did not claim a particular identifier.

This distinction is subtle and useful:

- An /automatic/ collision is expected document layout.
- An /explicit/ collision is conflicting author intent.

Both must produce unique output, but only one deserves a diagnostic.

{#file-local-identities}
### File-local identities

Reference-definition labels are different. Consider two files that both use
the convenient label `docs`:

```carve
%% parent.crv
Read [the parent documentation][docs].

[docs]: /parent
```

```carve
%% child.crv
Read [the child documentation][docs].

[docs]: /child
```

Those labels are authoring metadata rather than public document targets. Carve
resolves each reference within the file that defines it before merging the
child into its parent. Both files can keep the readable label `docs`, and each
link reaches its intended destination.

Making every label global would force reusable files to coordinate private
names. Renaming every label would create churn without protecting any public
identity. File-local scope is the more composable rule.

{#footnotes-after-assembly}
### Footnotes after assembly

Footnotes cross the file boundary in the other direction. A parent may refer to
a footnote supplied by a child, or a child may refer to a footnote defined by
the parent. Carve judges those references against the assembled document and
collects definitions the same way it would if the equivalent source had been
written in one file.

That ordering prevents a common AST-pipeline bug: parsing the child alone,
marking a footnote reference as permanently unresolved, and never revisiting it
after the documents are merged.

{#structure-is-identity-too}
## Structure is identity too

Identifiers are not the only cross-file concern. Heading levels determine the
outline, table of contents, section numbering, and often accessibility
navigation.

A reusable chapter might start with `# Installation`, while its include site is
already below `## Guide`. Copying the chapter verbatim produces a second
top-level section. Hard-coding lower headings in the child makes the file fit one
location but prevents reuse elsewhere.

Carve makes the choice explicit:

```carve
## Guide

{{ installation.crv @shift:auto }}
```

`@shift:auto` places the included content one level below the surrounding
heading context. A numeric shift is available when the author wants direct
control. Heading levels are clamped to the valid range with a warning, while
IDs and cross-references remain attached to their headings.

This is stronger than an ambient “normalize all includes” option. The include
site records the structural decision, so the same child can be embedded at
different depths intentionally.

{#how-other-markup-systems-compare}
## How other markup systems compare

The comparison is less about surface syntax than about which layer owns the
composition contract.

{#commonmark-and-gfm}
### CommonMark and GFM

[CommonMark](https://spec.commonmark.org/) does not define file inclusion.
GitHub Flavored Markdown does not turn an arbitrary include marker into
transclusion either. Products built on Markdown commonly add a template engine,
shortcode, or source preprocessor.

That is perfectly capable of producing a correct document if the host performs
a global semantic pass afterward. The deficit is portability: collision rules,
relative-path behavior, source provenance, and missing-dependency behavior
belong to the particular product rather than Markdown. Moving the same source
to another renderer can expose the directive as text or produce different IDs.

Pure text preprocessing has another trap. If expansion happens before parsing,
the final parser can usually de-duplicate automatic heading slugs globally. But
the preprocessor has lost the file boundary unless it records provenance. That
makes it difficult to preserve file-local reference labels or explain which
included file introduced an explicit collision.

If each file is parsed separately and ASTs are merged later, provenance is
easier to retain—but identifiers and references may already have been resolved
inside isolated documents. The host then needs a deliberate re-binding pass.

{#djot}
### Djot

[Djot](https://github.com/jgm/djot) specifies automatic heading identifiers and
numeric suffixes for uniqueness within a document. Its JavaScript implementation
also supports AST filters, which gives a host a strong foundation for custom
composition.

Djot does not currently specify a portable file-inclusion directive or a
cross-file collision contract. An application can preprocess Djot source or
recognize an application-specific marker and splice parsed AST nodes with a
filter. It must then choose when to generate heading IDs, how to scope reference
definitions, whether to re-bind footnotes, and how to retain source locations.

In other words, Djot can implement the same behavior. Different Djot hosts are
not required to implement the same behavior.

{#mdbook}
### mdBook

[mdBook](https://rust-lang.github.io/mdBook/format/mdbook.html#including-files)
adds `{{#include ...}}` through a preprocessor. It is especially effective for
including code files, line ranges, and named regions. The included material is
then interpreted as Markdown.

This is a practical host extension, but its contract is tailored to a book
builder rather than standardized Markdown. Document-global behavior depends on
the downstream Markdown renderer and the way mdBook divides chapters into
pages. An ID only needs to be unique within one HTML page, while a single-file
output format may create a wider collision domain.

{#quarto-and-pandoc}
### Quarto and Pandoc

[Quarto includes](https://quarto.org/docs/authoring/includes.html) are explicitly
equivalent to copying and pasting included text into the main file. The
documentation warns that relative references resolve from the main file and
that included metadata can have unexpected effects. Include shortcodes must
also occupy their own line, so they cannot be inserted into arbitrary inline or
list contexts.

Quarto’s own footnote documentation tells authors that footnote IDs must be
unique across chapters for formats that combine a book into one document. That
is an honest statement of the collision domain, but it leaves coordination to
authors rather than specifying rename-and-rebind behavior. Pandoc provides an
`identifier-prefix` option that is useful when producing fragments for later
inclusion, again requiring the pipeline or author to choose and carry a prefix.

These tools are powerful. Their trade-off is that identity management is a
build configuration concern rather than a property of the include directive.

{#what-carves-contract-adds}
## What Carve’s contract adds

Carve keeps the core parser pure: without a resolver, an include directive stays
literal. A host opts into transclusion by supplying a resolver. That resolver
may read a project file, query a database, call a CMS, load an object, or fetch
from an allowlisted online service.

Once a resolver is present, the include expansion has shared semantics across
implementations:

- Includes are recognized in grammar-aware positions, never inside verbatim
  code.
- Block and inline includes have distinct structural requirements.
- Children are parsed as whole documents and retain their provenance.
- Relative nested paths are resolved using the including resource’s identity.
- Explicit heading IDs and footnote labels are deterministically renamed on
  collision, and their references follow.
- Automatic heading slugs use ordinary document-wide de-duplication.
- Reference-definition labels remain file-local.
- Footnotes are re-bound against the assembled document.
- Heading shifts preserve target identity while adapting structure.
- Resolved and unresolved dependencies are reported to the host.
- Cycles, depth, resolver calls, and expanded bytes are bounded.
- Refused or unresolved directives remain visible with diagnostics rather than
  disappearing silently.

This does not mean Carve solves every possible global namespace automatically.
Extension-defined identities—citation keys, equation labels, diagram names,
component IDs, or application-specific anchors—need rules from that extension
or host. Likewise, the current inclusion collision contract specifically names
explicit heading IDs, automatic heading slugs, footnote labels, and reference
definitions; an application that emits arbitrary HTML `id` attributes from
other nodes must still guarantee their uniqueness.

That boundary is important. A language should standardize identities it owns,
and expose enough provenance and traversal hooks for extensions to standardize
theirs. Pretending every string called `id` has identical semantics would be as
wrong as pretending every label should be global.

{#a-checklist-for-any-transclusion-system}
## A checklist for any transclusion system

Whether the input is Carve, Djot, Markdown, or a proprietary format, an online
host should be able to answer all of the following before enabling reusable
content:

1. What is the canonical identity of an included resource?
2. Which namespaces are document-global, file-local, or extension-local?
3. How are duplicate explicit identifiers handled?
4. Are references rewritten when their target is renamed?
5. When are automatic slugs generated: before or after assembly?
6. Are footnotes, citations, and numbered objects resolved before or after
   assembly?
7. Are relative paths based on the parent, child, or a fixed project root?
8. Can diagnostics identify the original resource and position?
9. Does the dependency graph include failed lookup attempts?
10. Are cycles, depth, total bytes, calls, and remote latency bounded?
11. Does unresolved content remain visible and diagnosable?
12. Can the source be written back without flattening away its include
    directives?

If the answer is “the template engine pastes strings,” the system is not
finished. It may be sufficient for trusted fragments with no identifiers, but
it has not defined document composition.

{#the-real-feature}
## The real feature is deterministic composition

The most visible part of transclusion is the include directive. The most
valuable part is everything users do not have to repair after expansion.

A robust system preserves local meaning while creating one coherent final
document. It keeps identifiers unique, references connected, footnotes
resolvable, heading structure intentional, source locations useful, and failure
visible. It also makes these results deterministic across implementations.

That is the larger opportunity in Carve’s approach. The resolver keeps storage
and authority in the host, while the language contract defines what it means to
combine the resolved content. Markdown and Djot hosts can build equivalent
systems—and sophisticated ones do—but without a shared transclusion contract,
each ecosystem integration must rediscover the same identity problems and make
its own compatibility promises.

For the complete normative behavior, see Carve’s
[File inclusion documentation](https://markup-carve.github.io/carve/includes).
